 <section xml:id="Section-2">
      <title>Two Ways to Represent a <m>k</m>-Dimensional Plane</title>

      <p>
        Consider the plane
        <me>x+y+z = 0</me>
        A parametric solution to the system is the following:
        <me>f(y,z) = (-y-z,y,z) = y(-1,1,0) + z(-1,0,1). </me>
        In other words, the plane is <m>\text{span}((-1,1,0), (-1,0,1))</m>.
      </p>

      <p>The first is an equational or <alert>outer</alert> representation. It tells you what the plane is by giving you a way to test by plugging it into the equation, but it does not give you a way to generate any vector in the plane without solving a linear system.</p>

      <p>The second is a parametric or <alert>inner</alert> representation. This gives you an easy way to get vectors in the plane, but testing if a vector is in the plane corresponds to solving a linear system.</p>

      <p>We've already covered how to go from an outer representation to an inner. That's what Gaussian elimination does. But how does one go from an inner representation to an outer one?</p>

      <p>In concrete terms: Given a collection of vectors, how do we find an equation describing their span?</p>

      <p>
        This is where the dot product interpretation comes to help us. The key observation is: If a vector is in a row of our matrix representation it must dot product with each vector in the span to equal <m>0</m>. In other words, for <m>(-1,1,0)</m> and <m>(-1,0,1)</m> it must be a solution to the equation:
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\-1 \amp 0 \amp1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} x \\y\\z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0\\0 \end{pmatrix}.</me>
        We can solve this. The augmented matrix is
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp0 \amp 1 \amp0\end{pmatrix}.</me>
        Replacing row <m>1</m> with row <m>1</m> - row <m>2</m> yields
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp0 \amp 1 \amp0\end{pmatrix}.</me>
        Rescaling the second row by <m>-1</m> gives
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp0 \amp -1 \amp0\end{pmatrix}.</me>
        Then interchanging yields the reduced row echelon form:
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp1 \amp -1 \amp0\end{pmatrix}.</me>
        The first two columns are pivot columns, and the equations are <m>x-z= 0</m> and <m>y-z =0</m>. Thus, the solution is <m>f(z) = (z,z,z) = z(1,1,1)</m>, so the solution set is <m>\text{span}((1,1,1))</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        It turns out then that <m>(1,1,1)</m> is the row of the coefficient matrix in the equation defining <m>\text{span}((-1,1,0), (-1,0,1))</m> is defined by the matrix equation:
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} x\\y\\z \end{pmatrix} = 0.</me>
        In fact, that is how it always works. If you want to find an outer representation for the span of a set of vectors:
        <ul>
          <li><p>Put those vectors as rows of a matrix <m>A</m> in the equation <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Solve that equation.</p></li>
          <li><p>Write that solution as a span of vectors.</p></li>
          <li><p>Those vectors are the rows of the matrix <m>B</m> in the outer representation <m>B \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </section>